A=[]
def add(id,s,paras,title,kind,body,locator,quote=''):
 A.append(dict(id=id,source=s,paras=paras,title=title,kind=kind,body=body,locator=locator,quote=quote))
add('sale-nature','sale',[183,184],'Nature as a political instrument','Quotation','Sale reads the appeal to nature as instrumental rather than an expression of ecological understanding. He questions what the symbol does for the proposed ideology.','Paragraph beginning “In short, it feels to me…”','entirely utilitarian')
add('sale-core','sale',[187,188,189],'An intellectual cadre and its audience','Quotation','Sale brings the differentiated audiences together and compares the proposed political relationship to Leninism.','Paragraph beginning “But it doesn’t much matter to him…”',"Lenin couldn’t have put it better.")
add('sale-revolution','sale',[193],'An unresolved account of violence','Quotation','Sale juxtaposes the possibility of a non-armed revolution with the author’s actual bombings and destructive rhetoric. He finds the relation between means and ends unresolved.','Paragraphs beginning “He says that…” and “This is a somewhat peculiar position…”')
add('luke-unknown','luke',[180],'A different elite, the same uncertainty?','Quotation','Luke turns the charge of recklessness back on the proposed revolution: what would prevent its leaders from reproducing the uncertainty they condemn?','Paragraph beginning “In many ways, the Unabomber only seems committed…”','wise ecological fiat')
add('luke-destruction','luke',[182],'A decisively negative transition','Quotation','Luke reads the refusal to design a successor society alongside the commitment to revolutionary leadership. Destruction does not resolve the problem of who would exercise power.','Paragraph ending with the citation “(¶182)”')
add('luke-core','luke',[189],'Vanguardism beneath anti-politics','Quotation','Luke interprets the committed minority as a political vanguard. His criticism concerns the organization of authority, not simply the stated opposition to technology.','Paragraph beginning “The revolutionaries of the Unabomber’s Freedom Club…”','classic Bolshevik strategy')
add('luke-justice','luke',[200,201],'What gets sacrificed to the single goal?','Quotation','Luke connects the exclusion of social justice to the demand to eliminate organization-dependent technology. He questions the social arrangements implied by that priority.','Discussion of social justice as a revolutionary goal, ending with the citation (¶201)')
add('fulano-goal','fulano',[206],'A plurality of commitments','Quotation','Fulano directly contests the single-goal formulation. He presents commitments to people, place, and mutual aid as irreducible to technological destruction.','Concluding paragraph beginning “Connected to people and place…”','many complex, interrelated aims')
add('faun-nature','faun',[183,184],'An ideology made to recruit','Discussion','Feral Faun objects to the proposed opposition between wild nature and industrial technology as a manipulative ideology. The objection concerns its compatibility with autonomy.','Section “FC’s Fixed Idea #2”; paragraph beginning “Those who have a cause…”')
add('faun-audience','faun',[187,188],'Leaders and followers','Discussion','Feral Faun reads the propaganda strategy as producing a group in which a few lead and most follow. This is a conceptual connection to these paragraphs; no paragraph number is supplied in that passage.','Section “FC’s Fixed Idea #2”; discussion of propagandising and group dynamics')
add('faun-goal','faun',[200,206],'Whose life serves the cause?','Quotation','Feral Faun quotes the overriding-goal language and objects to subordinating individual life to a cause. Self-determination is the standard against which the proposed strategy is judged.','Section “FC’s Fixed Idea #2”; passage quoting the ONLY goal and no competing goal')
add('moore-nature','moore',[183],'Is nature outside ideology?','Quotation','Moore quotes §183 and argues that the opposition between nature and culture remains an ideological construction. Invoking wildness does not, in his reading, escape civilization’s categories.','“Ideology and strategy”; quotation introduced as Thesis 183')
add('moore-audience','moore',[187,188],'A hierarchy inside the manifesto','Quotation','Moore quotes §§187–188 together. He reads the distinction between sophisticated readers and an unthinking majority as reproducing hierarchy and class power.','“Ideology and strategy”; quotation introduced as Theses 187, 188','FC accept the hierarchical divisions of civilisation')
add('moore-goal','moore',[200,201,206],'Anti-authoritarianism under scrutiny','Quotation','Moore joins §§200, 201, and 206 in a composite quotation. He challenges the primacy of a single goal and the political authority implied by excluding other commitments.','“Ideology and strategy”; composite quotation introduced as Theses 200, 201, 206')
add('ur-destruction','ur',[182],'What does revolution mean here?','Explicit citation','UR cites §182, alongside §104, to dispute the claim that revolution necessarily entails designing a new society. The annotation records UR’s rebuttal, not an independent verification of ITS’s position.','Numbered point 4; paragraph explicitly citing 104 and 182')
add('ur-majority','ur',[189],'A paragraph used to correct another reading','Explicit citation','UR cites §189 against the position attributed to ITS that Kaczynski advocates educating the majority into opposition. The same paragraph criticized as vanguardist becomes evidence in a dispute over fidelity.','Numbered point 4; paragraph explicitly citing 189')
H={180:'utterly reckless ride into the unknown',182:'Our goal is only to destroy the existing form of society.',183:'The positive ideal that we propose is Nature.',184:'Nature makes a perfect counter-ideal to technology',187:'people who are intelligent, thoughtful and rational',188:'the unthinking majority',189:'a small core of deeply committed people',193:'will not necessarily involve an armed uprising',200:'the revolutionaries’ ONLY goal',201:'social justice',206:'the single, overriding goal'}
